---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { cpuSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
::selection{background:#fdcb6e;color:#2d3436}

.part5-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#fdcb6e 0%,#e17055 50%,#d63031 100%)}

.part5-num{color:#e17055;background:#fef5e7}

/* ── Hero Art: Python Snake ── */
.python-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0.75rem;animation:fadeInUp 1.5s ease}
.snake-body{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:4px}
.snake-segment{width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;border-radius:0.375rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.25);border:2px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.4)}
.snake-segment:nth-child(1){border-radius:0.625rem 0.375rem 0.375rem 0.625rem;width:1.75rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.35)}
.snake-segment:nth-child(1)::after{content:'';display:inline-block}
.snake-segment:nth-child(odd){animation:snakeWave 2s ease infinite}
.snake-segment:nth-child(even){animation:snakeWave 2s ease 0.3s infinite}
@keyframes snakeWave{0%,100%{transform:translateY(0)}50%{transform:translateY(-4px)}}
.python-label{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);letter-spacing:2px}

/* ── Interpreter Line-by-Line Animation ── */
.interp-demo{max-width:31.25rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1.5rem}
.interp-box{background:#2d3436;border-radius:1rem;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.8rem;line-height:2.2;color:#dfe6e9;position:relative;overflow:hidden}
.interp-line{position:relative;padding-left:1rem;opacity:0.4;transition:opacity 0.5s}
.interp-line.active{opacity:1}
.interp-line.active::before{content:'';position:absolute;left:0;top:4px;bottom:4px;width:4px;border-radius:2px;background:#fdcb6e;animation:interpGlow 1.5s ease infinite}
@keyframes interpGlow{0%,100%{opacity:0.5}50%{opacity:1}}
.interp-arrow{color:#fdcb6e;margin-left:0.5rem;font-size:0.7rem}
.interp-result{color:#00b894;margin-left:0.5rem;font-size:0.7rem}
.interp-caption{text-align:center;margin-top:0.75rem;font-size:0.8rem;color:#888;font-weight:700}

/* ── Pyc Compare ── */
.pyc-compare{max-width:43.75rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1.5rem}
.pyc-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1.25rem}
.pyc-card{border-radius:1rem;padding:1.5rem;text-align:center}
.pyc-card h4{font-size:1rem;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:0.5rem}
.pyc-card p{font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.6}
.pyc-card .pyc-flow{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.7rem;margin-top:0.75rem;padding:0.75rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.05);border-radius:0.625rem;line-height:1.8}
.pyc-java{background:#f0efff;border:2px solid #d5ccff}
.pyc-python{background:#fef5e7;border:2px solid #fdd9b5}
@media(max-width:37.5rem){.pyc-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}

/* ── Glue Language Illustration ── */
.glue-visual{max-width:31.25rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1.5rem}
.glue-box{background:linear-gradient(145deg,#1e1e2e,#2d3436);border-radius:1.25rem;padding:1.75rem;text-align:center;position:relative;box-shadow:0 12px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.3)}
.glue-layers{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.75rem;align-items:center}
.glue-layer{padding:0.75rem 1.75rem;border-radius:0.75rem;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;width:80%;max-width:17.5rem;position:relative}
.glue-py{background:linear-gradient(90deg,#fdcb6e,#e17055);color:#fff}
.glue-arrow-down{color:rgba(255,255,255,0.4);font-size:1.2rem}
.glue-c{background:linear-gradient(90deg,#00b894,#0984e3);color:#fff}
.glue-cpu{background:rgba(255,255,255,0.08);color:#b2bec3;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15)}
.glue-label{font-size:0.65rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.4);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:2px;margin-top:0.5rem}

/* ── Quiz theming: Episode 5 ── */
.quiz-ep5 .quiz-q-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep5 .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep5 .quiz-opt-letter{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep5 .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#00b894;background:#f0fdf9}
.quiz-ep5 .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#e8f8f5;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero part5-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 5 of 7</div>
  <h1>Python: One Bytecode<br>Instruction At A Time</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">No compile step to native binary. Python compiles to bytecode behind the scenes, then its interpreter dispatches each instruction to pre-compiled C handlers — no new machine code is ever produced.</p>

  <div class="python-art">
    <div class="snake-body">
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
      <div class="snake-segment"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="python-label">instruction by instruction</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 16: PYTHON -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num part5-num">16</span>
  <h2>The Simplest Path (and the Slowest)</h2>
  <p>In Episode 3, we saw how C compiles to binary before you run the program. In Episode 4, Java compiles to bytecode, then the JVM creates binary at runtime. Python takes a third approach — the simplest, and the slowest.</p>
  <p>There's no compile-to-native-binary step you ever see. You just run your code directly — Python quietly compiles it to bytecode first, then the interpreter takes over:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">python</span> hello.py

<span class="code-comment">That's it. No gcc. No javac. Just run.</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>A program called the <span class="highlight-orange">interpreter</span> (CPython) compiles your source to bytecode, then reads each bytecode instruction and runs the corresponding pre-compiled C handler function. No new native binary is ever produced.</p>
</section>

<!-- FLOW DIAGRAM -->
<div class="reveal" style="max-width:56.25rem;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1.5rem">
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-box fb-purple">hello.py<br><small>source</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-orange">CPython<br><small>compiler</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-purple">.pyc<br><small>bytecode</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-dark">Interpreter<br><small>opcode by opcode</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-green">CPU<br><small>via C handlers</small></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- INTERPRETER DEMO -->
<div class="interp-demo reveal">
  <div class="interp-box">
    <div class="interp-line active">
      name = "Gokul"
      <span class="interp-arrow">&#10132;</span>
      <span class="interp-result">CPU stores "Gokul" in memory</span>
    </div>
    <div class="interp-line">
      print("Hello " + name)
      <span class="interp-arrow">&#10132;</span>
      <span class="interp-result">CPU prints "Hello Gokul"</span>
    </div>
    <div class="interp-line">
      <span style="color:#636e72">Done. No binary file created.</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="interp-caption">CPython dispatches each bytecode opcode to a C handler — no new machine code is produced</div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p><strong>So when does Python code become binary?</strong> It doesn't, in the usual sense. CPython's interpreter reads each bytecode instruction and calls the corresponding pre-compiled C function — the C runtime itself is native binary, but no new machine code is generated for your Python code. Nothing is compiled, cached, or reused on your behalf.</p>
  <p>And just like Java needs the JVM, Python needs the <span class="highlight-orange">Python interpreter</span> installed on the machine. Without it, <code>.py</code> files are just text files — the computer doesn't know what to do with them.</p>
</section>

<!-- .PYC SECTION -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <h3 style="font-size:1.3rem;color:#e17055">Wait — What About .pyc Files?</h3>
  <p>If you've used Python, you may have noticed a <code>__pycache__</code> folder with <code>.pyc</code> files appearing next to your code. That looks like compilation — and it is, partially.</p>
  <p>When you run <code>hello.py</code>, Python actually does a hidden step first: it compiles your source code into <span class="highlight">bytecode</span> — similar to what Java does. This bytecode is saved as a <code>.pyc</code> file.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">hello.py</span> <span class="code-arrow">→</span> <span class="code-string">Python compiler</span> <span class="code-arrow">→</span> <span class="code-string">hello.cpython-312.pyc</span> <span class="code-comment">(bytecode)</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Sound familiar? It's the same idea as Java's <code>.class</code> files. But here's the critical difference:</p>
</section>

<!-- PYC vs JAVA COMPARE -->
<div class="pyc-compare reveal">
  <div class="pyc-grid">
    <div class="pyc-card pyc-java">
      <h4 style="color:#6c5ce7">Java</h4>
      <p>bytecode &rarr; JVM with JIT &rarr; <strong>native binary</strong> (fast, cached)</p>
      <div class="pyc-flow">
        .class &rarr; JVM<br>
        &darr;<br>
        JIT compiles hot paths<br>
        &darr;<br>
        <span style="color:#00b894">native binary &rarr; CPU</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pyc-card pyc-python">
      <h4 style="color:#e17055">Python</h4>
      <p>bytecode &rarr; CPython interpreter &rarr; <strong>C handlers, instruction by instruction</strong> (slow, no JIT)</p>
      <div class="pyc-flow">
        .pyc &rarr; CPython<br>
        &darr;<br>
        dispatches C handler per opcode<br>
        &darr;<br>
        <span style="color:#e17055">no new native binary generated</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>Java's JVM takes bytecode and <span class="highlight">compiles hot paths into native binary</span> that the CPU runs directly. Python's interpreter (called CPython) <span class="highlight-orange">dispatches each bytecode instruction to a pre-compiled C handler function</span> — no JIT, no new machine code generated.</p>
  <p>So the <code>.pyc</code> file saves Python from re-parsing your source code every time — this meaningfully speeds up import and startup time. But bytecode execution speed is unchanged; instructions are still handled one by one. The bytecode never becomes native binary.</p>
</section>

<div class="analogy reveal">
  <p>It's like translating a book from English to a simpler English — it's easier to read, but you're still reading it <strong>word by word</strong> instead of memorizing it.</p>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>That's why Python is still much slower than Java, despite both having a bytecode step. This makes Python the <span class="highlight-orange">slowest</span> of the four languages we're covering.</p>
</section>

<!-- WHY USE PYTHON -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <h3 style="font-size:1.3rem;color:#00b894">So Why Would Anyone Use It?</h3>
  <p>Because writing Python is <span class="highlight-green">incredibly fast for humans</span>:</p>
</section>

<div class="compare reveal">
  <div class="compare-card cc-blue">
    <span class="emoji">&#9881;</span>
    <h3>C (read a file)</h3>
    <div style="text-align:left;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.7rem;line-height:1.8;margin-top:0.75rem;color:#2d3436">
      FILE *f = fopen("data.txt", "r");<br>
      if (f == NULL) { return 1; }<br>
      char buffer[1024];<br>
      while (fgets(buffer, 1024, f)) {<br>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;printf("%s", buffer);<br>
      }<br>
      fclose(f);
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;font-weight:800;color:#636e72">7 lines</p>
  </div>
  <div class="compare-card cc-red">
    <span class="emoji">&#128013;</span>
    <h3>Python (same thing)</h3>
    <div style="text-align:left;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.85rem;line-height:1.8;margin-top:2rem;color:#2d3436">
      print(open("data.txt").read())
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:2rem;font-weight:800;color:#636e72">1 line</p>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>What takes 7 lines in C takes 1 line in Python. For web apps, data analysis, automation scripts — <span class="highlight">developer time matters more than CPU time</span>.</p>
</section>

<!-- GLUE LANGUAGE -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <h3 style="font-size:1.3rem;color:#6c5ce7">The Hidden Truth: Python Is a Glue Language</h3>
  <p>Here's something most beginners don't realize: when Python does heavy work, <span class="highlight-orange">it's not actually Python doing the work</span>. The heavy lifting happens in libraries written in C.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">import</span> numpy <span class="code-keyword">as</span> np
data = np.array([1, 2, 3])
result = data * 2

<span class="code-comment">That multiplication doesn't run in Python.</span>
<span class="code-comment">NumPy is written in C.</span>
<span class="code-comment">Python calls the C code → binary → CPU runs at full speed.</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- GLUE ILLUSTRATION -->
<div class="glue-visual reveal">
  <div class="glue-box">
    <div class="glue-layers">
      <div class="glue-layer glue-py">Python &mdash; writes the logic</div>
      <span class="glue-arrow-down">&darr; calls</span>
      <div class="glue-layer glue-c">C / CUDA &mdash; does the math</div>
      <span class="glue-arrow-down">&darr; binary</span>
      <div class="glue-layer glue-cpu">CPU / GPU &mdash; runs at full speed</div>
    </div>
    <div class="glue-label">Python is the glue holding it together</div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="section reveal">
  <p>That's why AI and machine learning use Python — you write training loops in Python, but the actual matrix multiplications run in C/CUDA libraries at full binary speed on GPUs. <span class="highlight">Python is just the glue</span> holding it all together.</p>
</section>

<!-- PYTHON GETTING FASTER -->
<div class="analogy reveal">
  <p><strong>Python is getting faster.</strong> Python 3.13+ introduced an experimental <span class="highlight">JIT compiler</span> — just like Java's. It watches for hot code paths and compiles them to binary. It's early and disabled by default: it requires a special build flag (<code>--enable-experimental-jit</code>) and a runtime flag (<code>PYTHON_JIT=1</code>). Standard Python distributions don't include it yet, but the speed gap between Python and Java is starting to close.</p>
</div>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#fef5e7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:4px solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">CPython dispatches, not translates</strong> — the interpreter reads each bytecode instruction and calls the corresponding pre-compiled C handler function. No new native binary is ever produced. No JIT, no cached machine code.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:4px solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">.pyc files speed up startup</strong> — they save re-parsing source code, which meaningfully reduces import and startup time. Bytecode execution speed itself is unchanged. Both Java and Python compile to bytecode — the difference is what happens next.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:4px solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Python is a glue language</strong> — the heavy work runs in C libraries at native binary speed. Python just orchestrates it. That's why AI uses Python.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#ffeef7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:4px solid #e84393">
      <strong style="color:#e84393">Slowest to run, fastest to write</strong> — developer time matters more than CPU time for most tasks. Python 3.13+ has an experimental JIT (disabled by default) that could close the gap over time.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep5">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What happens to the native binary after CPython executes a bytecode instruction?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>It's saved to a .pyc file for next time</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>It's cached in memory like Java's JIT</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>No native binary is produced — CPython runs a pre-compiled C handler for that opcode</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>It's stored in the __pycache__ folder</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) No new native binary is produced — CPython runs pre-compiled C handlers</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">CPython's interpreter dispatches each bytecode opcode to a pre-compiled C handler function. No new machine code is ever generated. Unlike Java's JIT, nothing is compiled or cached — the C runtime handles each instruction every single time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Why is Python slow but still one of the most popular languages?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Python is actually faster than C in most cases</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Developer time matters more than CPU time — Python is incredibly fast to write</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Python uses GPU acceleration by default</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Python's interpreter was recently rewritten in Rust</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Developer time matters more than CPU time — Python is incredibly fast to write</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Python trades CPU speed for developer speed. What takes 50 lines in C takes 5 in Python. For most applications, the bottleneck is developer productivity, not CPU cycles.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">How does Python achieve high performance in data science and AI?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Python's interpreter is specially optimized for math</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Python compiles data science code differently</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Libraries like NumPy call into fast C code — Python is just the glue</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Python switches to JIT mode for numerical operations</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Libraries like NumPy call into fast C code — Python is just the glue</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">When you call NumPy or TensorFlow, Python hands off the work to highly optimized C/C++/CUDA code. Python orchestrates; the heavy lifting happens in compiled libraries.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header">
      <span class="quiz-q-num">Q 04</span>
    </div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What recent change is making Python itself faster?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Python is being rewritten in Rust</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Python now compiles to native binary like C</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Python 3.13+ introduced an experimental JIT compiler</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Python switched from bytecode to direct interpretation</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Python 3.13+ introduced an experimental JIT compiler</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">CPython 3.13 added an experimental JIT compiler that watches for hot paths and compiles them to native binary. It's disabled by default and requires special build and runtime flags — standard Python distributions don't ship with it yet. But it's a meaningful step toward closing the speed gap.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT EPISODE -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#fdcb6e,#e17055)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 6?</h2>
  <p>JavaScript takes the most unique path of all — it ships source code directly to your browser. And we'll see all four languages side by side for the complete picture.</p>
  <a href="part6.html" class="btn btn-white" style="color:#e17055">Continue to Episode 6 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How Your Code Runs — Episode 5: Python — One Bytecode Instruction At A Time | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={cpuSeries} episodeNum={5} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
